<TopicPage slug="subjects/religious-studies/intermediate/christianity">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Christianity is the world's largest religion (~2.4 billion) — <strong>founded on the
    resurrection claim, organised around scripture and sacrament, and split historically into
    Catholic, Orthodox and Protestant branches</strong>. Its 2,000-year arc runs from a persecuted
    sect to global diversity: the study tracks how one message was translated across every
    culture it entered.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the claim, the ascent, the schisms, and the canon" widget="equationStep" config={christCfg} />

  <h2>What the study emphasises</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Historical-critical study of the gospels:</strong> Mark written ~70 CE, Matthew and Luke using Mark plus a lost source ("Q" hypothesis), John last and most theological — the texts have histories, and reading them historically changed theology itself.</li>
    <li><strong>Augustine shaped Western everything:</strong> original sin, grace, just war — his synthesis (4th–5th c.) underlies Western thought's defaults, secular and religious alike.</li>
    <li><strong>Monotheism with a Trinity:</strong> the doctrine (one God, three persons) is the tradition's most distinctive and most debated metaphysical claim — the great councils were largely grammatical emergencies about it.</li>
    <li><strong>Global shift:</strong> Christianity's centre of gravity moved south — most Christians now live in Africa, Latin America and Asia; Pentecostalism is the fastest-growing movement. The "Western" religion is no longer Western in composition.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> the New Testament (NRSV); Chadwick, The Early Church; MacCulloch, Christianity: The First Three Thousand Years (2009).</p>
</TopicPage>
